## Context

Ticket 0610 (PR #1274) set out to regenerate three artifacts assumed stale from
the 0591 producer change, and found the real cause was different: the committed
copies predated the corpus that `dvc.lock` now pins. The same drift was then
found by accident in `tab_network_limitations.csv` (ticket 0625), whose numbers
were quoted verbatim to RDJ26561 reviewers. Two live instances found without
looking means the class deserves one systematic pass.

## Relevant files

- `deliverables/_shared/tables/`, `deliverables/_shared/figures/`,
  `deliverables/_shared/_includes/` — git-tracked Phase-2 outputs
- `scripts/analysis/*.mk`, `paths.mk` — the producers and their wiring

## Actions

1. In a corpus-present worktree (`make data`), rebuild every git-tracked
   Phase-2 artifact from the current pinned corpus.
2. Diff each against its committed copy; classify clean / cosmetic /
   substantive.
3. For substantive diffs, check the prose that includes or quotes the artifact
   (manuscript, tech report, data paper, revision responses) before committing.
4. Commit the regenerated set with the classification in the message. Anything
   touching a submission record is reported to the author, not auto-committed
   (precedent: 0625).

## Test

No red step — regeneration audit; determinism of the producers is already
pinned by `tests/test_hash_seed_determinism.py` and `make determinism-check`.

## Verification

- [ ] Every tracked Phase-2 artifact rebuilt and diffed
- [ ] Each diff classified; substantive ones reconciled with citing prose
- [ ] Submission-record artifacts left for author arbitration

## Exit criteria

All tracked Phase-2 artifacts match what their producers emit from the pinned
corpus, or carry an explicit author decision to stay as committed.
